    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Texas HOA laws give homeowners more protection than many people expect. Chapter 209 of the Texas Property Code, the Texas Residential Property Owners Protection Act, requires most homeowners associations to open their records and board meetings, send certified notice before fining you, offer payment plans and get a court order before foreclosing. Chapter 202 adds a list of things your HOA cannot ban outright, from solar panels to rain barrels.</p>
<p>Here is what the law says as of 2026, including the changes that took effect in 2025, and how Houston-area homeowners can use it.</p>

<h2>Who Texas HOA laws apply to</h2>
<p>Chapter 209 applies to residential subdivisions where owners are automatically members of a property owners' association that can levy mandatory assessments. That covers most HOAs in master-planned communities across Harris, Fort Bend and Montgomery counties. Condominiums are governed mainly by Chapter 82 instead, and some rules apply differently while a developer still controls the association.</p>
<p>Your association's own documents (the declaration of covenants, bylaws and rules, called dedicatory instruments) still matter. But where a document conflicts with Chapter 209 or 202, the statute generally controls.</p>

<h2>Records access</h2>
<p>Under §209.005, you can inspect or copy association records, including books, financial records, contracts and minutes. To use this right:</p>
<ol>
<li>Send a written request by certified mail to the address on the association's most recent management certificate.</li>
<li>Describe the records in enough detail and say whether you want to inspect them first or get copies.</li>
<li>Expect a response within 10 business days. If the HOA needs more time, it must tell you in writing by then and set a date no later than 15 business days later.</li>
</ol>
<p>The association may charge reasonable costs, but only under a records production policy it has recorded, and the rates cannot exceed the state's standard charges. It may withhold other owners' violation histories, personal financial and contact information, employee files and attorney-client communications.</p>
<p>Associations with more than 14 lots must keep financial records, tax returns and meeting minutes for seven years and governing documents permanently. If the HOA refuses, you can send a pre-suit notice and then file in justice court, where the judge can order the records released and award attorney fees.</p>

<h2>Open board meetings</h2>
<p>Under §209.0051, board meetings are open to owners. Notice must give the date, time, place and general subject and can be given either by mail 10 to 60 days before the meeting, or by posting on common property or the HOA's website and emailing registered owners at least 144 hours before a regular meeting and 72 hours before a special meeting.</p>
<p>The board can meet in closed executive session only for specific matters such as personnel, pending litigation, contract negotiations, enforcement actions, attorney advice and owner privacy. Any decision made there must be summarized in the minutes.</p>
<p>Some actions always require an open, noticed meeting, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Levying fines or damage assessments.</li>
<li>Starting foreclosure or enforcement actions (other than emergency relief).</li>
<li>Raising assessments or levying a special assessment.</li>
<li>Adopting or amending governing documents or the annual budget.</li>
<li>Hearing appeals of denied architectural requests.</li>
<li>Borrowing money, buying or selling real property and filling board vacancies.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Notice and a hearing before fines</h2>
<p>Section 209.006 is the rule most homeowners need. Before an HOA fines you, suspends your common-area privileges, charges you for property damage or reports a delinquency to a credit bureau, it must send written notice by certified mail that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Describes the violation and any amount owed.</li>
<li>Gives you a reasonable period to cure it, if it is curable and not a threat to public health or safety.</li>
<li>Tells you that you may request a hearing before the board within 30 days after the notice was mailed.</li>
<li>Notes relief available to owners on active military duty.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you cure the violation within the cure period, no fine may be assessed. Curable violations include parking, maintenance and ongoing noise. Uncurable ones, such as a one-time event or property damage, can be fined without a cure period.</p>
<h3>How the hearing works (§209.007)</h3>
<p>Once you request a hearing in writing, the board must hold it within 30 days of receiving your request and give you at least 10 days' notice. At least 10 days before the hearing, the association must give you a packet of the documents, photos and communications it plans to use, or you get an automatic 15-day postponement. Either side may record the hearing, and each side can request one postponement of up to 10 days.</p>

<h2>Payment plans, collections and foreclosure limits</h2>
<p>Fall behind on dues and Chapter 209 still protects you.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment plans (§209.0062).</strong> Associations with more than 14 lots must adopt payment plan guidelines. The minimum term is three months. The HOA does not have to offer a plan to an owner who defaulted on one in the past two years or who already used one in the last 12 months.</li>
<li><strong>Before collections (§209.0064).</strong> You are not liable for a collection agent's fees unless the HOA first sends certified-mail notice itemizing what you owe, describing your options including a payment plan, and giving you at least 45 days to pay.</li>
<li><strong>Payment order (§209.0063).</strong> Partial payments generally go first to delinquent assessments, then current assessments, then collection costs and attorney fees, and only then to fines.</li>
</ul>
<p>Foreclosure is restricted in three ways. First, under §209.009, an HOA cannot foreclose if the debt consists only of fines, attorney fees associated solely with fines, or certain records charges. Second, under §209.0092, the association generally needs a court order through expedited foreclosure or a court judgment, rather than a quick nonjudicial sale. Third, under §209.011, the owner can redeem the home within 180 days after the association mails written notice of the sale by paying the amounts the statute lists.</p>
<p>Foreclosure law is technical, so talk to a Texas real estate attorney as soon as you receive a demand letter.</p>

<h2>What Texas HOAs cannot prohibit</h2>
<p>Chapter 202 voids or limits HOA bans on a list of items. Associations can usually still require approval and set reasonable rules on placement and appearance.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Statute</th><th>What the HOA can still regulate</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Drought-resistant landscaping, water-conserving turf, composting, efficient irrigation</td><td>§202.007</td><td>Plan review for aesthetic compatibility, but approval cannot be unreasonably withheld</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain barrels and rainwater harvesting</td><td>§202.007</td><td>Can bar them between the front of the house and the street, and limit colors and visible size</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brown grass during watering restrictions (new in 2025)</td><td>§202.008</td><td>No fines during a residential watering restriction or for 60 days after it is lifted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar panels and solar roof tiles</td><td>§202.010</td><td>Roofline, designated area, frame color, fence height and prior approval</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind and hail resistant, energy-efficient or solar shingles</td><td>§202.011</td><td>Must resemble and match approved shingles in the subdivision</td></tr>
<tr><td>U.S., Texas and armed forces flags</td><td>§202.012</td><td>Flag size, lighting, and flagpole number and placement; at least one pole up to 20 feet must be allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Religious displays</td><td>§202.018</td><td>Only items that threaten safety, break other laws, are patently offensive for non-religious reasons, or sit on common property or easements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Permanent standby generators</td><td>§202.019</td><td>Licensed installation, screening, testing times and location, within cost limits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security measures such as cameras, lights and fencing</td><td>§202.023</td><td>Since 2025, can bar fences that block sidewalks or drainage easements and some front-yard fences</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Other Chapter 202 sections protect swimming pool safety enclosures and children's beverage stands. If your HOA cites a rule that conflicts with this list, ask in writing which statute exception it relies on.</p>

<h2>Management certificates and resale certificates</h2>
<p>Every Chapter 209 association must record a management certificate in the county records, listing the association's name, mailing address, the manager's contact information and the website for its governing documents. Since SB 1588 in 2021, associations must also file it electronically with the Texas Real Estate Commission (TREC) within seven days, on TREC's public HOA search site. Under §209.004, owners are not liable for delinquent-assessment interest or collection attorney fees while a required certificate is unrecorded.</p>
<p>Buyers rely on the resale certificate. Under §207.003, an owner, buyer or title company can request it, and the association must deliver it, along with the restrictions, bylaws and rules, within 10 business days. It discloses assessments and amounts past due, reserves, the budget, pending lawsuits, known violations and every fee charged on transfer. The fee is limited to a reasonable and necessary amount, and no fee may be charged if the certificate is late. Add those transfer fees to your estimate with our <a href="/tools/closing-cost-calculator/">closing cost calculator</a>.</p>
