<h2>2025 changes to Texas HOA laws</h2>
<p>The 89th Legislature passed several HOA bills, most effective September 1, 2025:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>SB 711:</strong> Architectural review committee seats must first go to owners who respond to a solicitation notice sent at least 10 days before selection. It also expanded what security fencing HOAs may restrict, with exceptions for fences installed before September 1, 2025 and for owners with documented safety needs.</li>
<li><strong>SB 2586:</strong> Associations must file their dedicatory instruments and enforcement policy electronically with TREC within seven days of filing a management certificate. Associations with certificates recorded by December 1, 2025 have until June 1, 2026. Owners are not liable for assessments, fines or fees levied while an association is out of compliance.</li>
<li><strong>SB 2629:</strong> HOAs must let owners vote by at least one of electronic ballot, absentee ballot or proxy.</li>
<li><strong>HB 517:</strong> No fines for brown or discolored grass during watering restrictions and for 60 days afterward.</li>
<li><strong>HB 621:</strong> Owners may invite government officials and candidates to meet in common areas, subject to normal reservation rules.</li>
<li><strong>HB 431:</strong> Solar roof tiles now count as protected solar energy devices.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to dispute an HOA decision and where to complain</h2>
<p>No state agency regulates HOAs in Texas. TREC hosts the management certificate database but says it has no regulatory authority over associations. That means you enforce your rights yourself:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Read the documents.</strong> Pull your declaration, bylaws and rules from the association's website or the county records.</li>
<li><strong>Respond in writing.</strong> Answer violation letters by email and certified mail, keep copies and photograph any cure.</li>
<li><strong>Request a hearing</strong> within 30 days of a violation notice.</li>
<li><strong>Request records</strong> to see how the rule has been enforced against others.</li>
<li><strong>Use alternative dispute resolution.</strong> Chapter 209 allows mediation, and either side can ask a court to compel it in certain suits.</li>
<li><strong>Go to court if needed.</strong> Records disputes can go to justice court. Larger disputes need a private attorney.</li>
<li><strong>Change it from the inside.</strong> Attend meetings, vote, and volunteer for the board or architectural committee.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is general information, not legal advice, so confirm your situation with a Texas real estate attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Texas HOA laws require your association to open its books, meet in public, warn you by certified mail and hear you out before fining you, offer a payment plan and get a court order before foreclosing. They also stop HOAs from banning solar panels, rain barrels, flags, generators and drought-tolerant yards. Buying in an HOA community? Read the resale certificate and seller disclosures closely, see our <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice guide</a>, and check whether the subdivision also sits in a <a href="/blog/mud-tax-texas/">MUD</a>.</p>
